title:    terraform destroy succeeded. The key is still there.
subtitle: Exit code zero, empty state, and a key ring the provider said could not be deleted
width:    1000
---
$ terraform destroy -auto-approve
Destroy complete!

$ terraform state list | wc -l
0

$ gcloud kms keys list --keyring wk12-secrets --location global

NAME                  PURPOSE          ROTATION_PERIOD  DESTROY_SCHEDULED_DURATION
wk12-app-db-password  ENCRYPT_DECRYPT                   86400s

$ gcloud kms keys versions list --key wk12-app-db-password

NAME  STATE              CREATE_TIME                 DESTROY_TIME
1     DESTROY_SCHEDULED  2026-11-05T04:50:51Z        2026-11-07T19:52:07Z

$ gcloud kms keys delete wk12-app-db-password --keyring wk12-secrets
ERROR: (gcloud.kms.keys.delete) INVALID_ARGUMENT: CryptoKeys can only be
deleted if all associated CryptoKeyVersions have already been deleted.
  reason: CRYPTOKEY_HAS_NON_DELETED_KEY_VERSIONS
exit code: 1

# Everything else really did go: 0 secrets, 0 topics, 0 subscriptions,
# 0 functions, 0 service accounts, 0 buckets. Terraform removed them and
# emptied its state. The key ring and the key remain, and the provider's own
# documentation says they will:
#
#   "CryptoKeys cannot be deleted from Google Cloud Platform ... will not
#    delete the resource from the project."
#
# Note the ROTATION_PERIOD column is now EMPTY. Terraform removed the
# automatic rotation schedule on its way out - which is one of Google's own
# preconditions for deleting a key. Part of the chain ran; the rest cannot.
#
# DESTROY_TIME is 24 hours away, not 30 days, only because this key set
# destroy_scheduled_duration to 86400s. On the default it would read
# 2026-12-05 - and a DESTROY_SCHEDULED version is a BILLED version the whole
# time. Teardown reported success; the meter did not stop.
